    <!-- third section - more info -->
    <section class="section-3">
        <div class="page_container">
            <p class="ex">
                But there is a slight ambiguity. There are angles that we can choose, ones outside \( \pm \)180 degrees
                and ones inside \( \pm \)180 degrees. If we want to always receive the small angle in between, how can
                we handle that? A little bit of trivial: that particular angle is called the conjugate angle. Sounds
                familiar right?
                <br>
                <br> If we look at the complex conjugate of Euler’s famous formula, we have $$Z = e^{ix} = cos(x) + i
                sin(x) \qquad \overline{Z} = e^{-ix} = cos(x) - isin(x).$$ Let \(x = 2 \pi - y\). We have then
                $$\overline{Z} = e ^{- i ( 2\pi - y)} = e ^{-2\pi i} * e ^{iy} = cos(2\pi - y) - i(sin(2\pi - y))$$ Z is
                cyclical such that for \(n \in \mathbb{Z} \) $$ e ^{-in\pi} = \begin{cases} \space \space \space 1,&
                \text{n is even} \\ -1, & \text{n is odd} \end{cases} $$ With this knowledge and the trigonometric
                identities $$cos(x) = cos(2\pi - y) = cos(-y) = cos(y) \qquad sin(x) = sin(2\pi - y) = sin(-y) = -
                sin(y) $$ we get \( e^{iy} = cos(y) + i sin (y) = e^{-ix} \) hence the etymology of the conjugate angle.
                <br>
                <br> Another way of looking at this is to have the complex number flipped over the real number line. The
                quadrants above the x-axis contains all the angles below 180 degrees and the quadrant below contains the
                angles above 180 degrees, given we're looking at \(0 \leq x \leq 2\pi\).
            </p>
